const {H1,H2,H3,P,STORY,CASE,TIP,DIG,SEC,LN,PB,TBL}=require("./lib");
const c=[];

// ---------- EP 01 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 01 — The Banker Is Not a Trustee: Foley v Hill and the Relationship"));
c.push(H2("Covers: What banking is (Sec 5(b) Banking Regulation Act) · Who is a customer · The general relationship — debtor and creditor · The special relationships — agent, bailee, trustee, lessor · The banker's duties (secrecy, honouring cheques, care) and rights (lien, set-off, charges) · The customer's duties"));
c.push(STORY("London, 1848. Edward Foley deposited six thousand one hundred and seventeen pounds with the bankers Hill and Company in 1829, on a promise of three per cent interest. For six years he never asked for the money. Then he sued in the Court of Chancery for an account, saying the bank was his trustee or agent and must account to him for what it had done with his money. The House of Lords, through Lord Cottenham, said no: money paid into a bank ceases to be the customer's money; it becomes the banker's, who may do with it as he pleases — lend it, invest it, put it at risk — and is answerable only to repay an equivalent sum on demand. The relationship is that of debtor and creditor, not trustee and beneficiary. Foley's claim was a simple debt, and a debt six years old was barred by limitation. That single decision explains almost everything about a bank: why a bank can lend your deposit, why a bank can fail, why the bank need not tell you what it did with the money, why your savings are a loan you have made and an overdraft is a loan you have taken, why interest is paid, and why the depositors of a failed bank are unsecured creditors who stand in a queue. Everything else in the law of banking — the special relationships of agent and bailee, the duty of secrecy, the right of lien — is built on the foundation that the money is the banker's and the customer has only a promise."));
c.push(H3("What banking is"));
c.push(P("**Sec 5(b) of the Banking Regulation Act, 1949:** 'banking' means the accepting, for the purpose of lending or investment, of deposits of money from the public, repayable on demand or otherwise, and withdrawable by cheque, draft, order or otherwise; **Sec 5(c):** a 'banking company' is a company which transacts the business of banking in India; the Explanation excludes a manufacturing or trading company that accepts deposits merely to finance its own business. The definition has three limbs — deposits from the public, for the purpose of lending or investment, and withdrawable by cheque or otherwise — and Sec 7 forbids any company other than a banking company from using the words 'bank', 'banker' or 'banking' in its name, and requires every banking company to use one of them. Sec 6 lists the forms of business a banking company may engage in besides banking — borrowing, lending, discounting bills, dealing in bullion and foreign exchange, safe deposit vaults, guarantees, agency business, acting as trustee — and Sec 8 prohibits trading. The English definition — United Dominions Trust v. Kirkwood (1966): the characteristics are accepting money from and collecting cheques for customers, honouring cheques drawn on it, and keeping current accounts — matches. In India the Reserve Bank of India Act, 1934 and the Banking Regulation Act, 1949 (originally the Banking Companies Act) together with the State Bank of India Act 1955, the Banking Companies (Acquisition and Transfer of Undertakings) Acts 1970 and 1980, the Regional Rural Banks Act 1976, the Co-operative Societies Acts, the Negotiable Instruments Act 1881, the Bankers' Books Evidence Act 1891 and the Recovery of Debts and Bankruptcy Act 1993 make up the statutory framework."));
c.push(H3("Who is a customer"));
c.push(P("The Negotiable Instruments Act uses the word (Sec 131) without defining it. The duration theory — that a person becomes a customer only after a course of dealing (Mathews v. Williams, Brown and Co., 1894) — was rejected in **Commissioners of Taxation v. English, Scottish and Australian Bank (1920)**: a person becomes a customer the moment the bank agrees to open an account for him, even if only one cheque has been paid in; and in **Ladbroke v. Todd (1914)** a thief who opened an account with a stolen cheque was a customer from the opening of the account. Two conditions: an account of some sort (deposit or current), and the account opened in the ordinary course of banking business — casual services such as encashing a cheque for a stranger do not make him a customer (Great Western Railway v. London and County Bank, 1901). Indian courts follow the same view (Central Bank of India v. Gopinathan Nair, 1979 Kerala — a person for whom the bank collects cheques on an account is a customer). Whether a person who has only a fixed deposit, or a locker, or a loan account is a 'customer' for Sec 131 is doubtful; for the general law of banker and customer, any person having an account is."));
c.push(H3("The general relationship — debtor and creditor"));
c.push(P("**Foley v. Hill (1848)** and **Joachimson v. Swiss Bank Corporation (1921)** settle the essentials: when a customer deposits money the bank becomes his **debtor** and he its **creditor**; when the bank lends, the positions reverse; the bank's obligation is to repay on demand at the branch where the account is kept, during banking hours (Joachimson — the demand is a condition precedent to the debt becoming payable, so limitation does not run until a demand is made; in India the Limitation Act, Art 22, gives three years from the demand for a deposit payable on demand); the debt is not held in trust (so the bank need not account for its use, and the customer has no proprietary claim on the bank's insolvency); the bank may set off; and the bank must honour cheques drawn against sufficient funds. Indian recognition: **Shanti Prasad Jain v. Director of Enforcement (1962)** — 'the relationship between a banker and a customer is one of debtor and creditor and not of trustee and beneficiary'; New Bank of India v. Union of India (1996); the Supreme Court in **Canara Bank v. Canara Sales Corporation (1987)** described the relationship as contractual, the bank being bound to act only on the customer's mandate. Special features distinguishing it from an ordinary loan: the creditor must demand; the debtor's obligation to honour cheques; the bank's duty of secrecy; the bank's right of lien and set-off; the customer's right to a statement of account. The **essential features** the examiner expects: (1) contractual; (2) debtor-creditor as the core; (3) demand as a condition of repayment; (4) place and time of payment; (5) the bank's right to use the money; (6) the bank's obligations arising from the account — cheques, secrecy, statements; (7) mutual rights of lien, set-off and charges."));
c.push(H3("The special relationships"));
c.push(SEC("Agent and principal","When the bank collects cheques, bills or dividends for the customer, pays his standing orders, or buys and sells securities on his instructions, it acts as agent (Sec 182 Contract Act) and must exercise the care of a reasonably competent banker (Sec 211-212); the proceeds of collection become a debt once credited. Sec 131 NI Act protection for the collecting banker is the statutory counterpart."));
c.push(SEC("Bailee and bailor","Articles received for safe custody — the locker or the sealed packet — make the bank a bailee (Sec 148 Contract Act), bound to take as much care as a man of ordinary prudence takes of his own goods (Sec 151) and liable for loss caused by its negligence; the locker is often structured as a lease of a compartment rather than a bailment, so that the bank does not know the contents — but the Supreme Court in **Amitabha Dasgupta v. United Bank of India (2021)** directed the RBI to frame rules and held that banks cannot escape liability for negligent operation of lockers by calling the relationship a lease; the RBI's 2021 locker guidelines cap liability at one hundred times the annual rent for the bank's own negligence, fraud or fire, and require agreements in the prescribed form."));
c.push(SEC("Trustee and beneficiary","Where money is deposited for a specific purpose which the bank has agreed to carry out — to pay a particular person, to meet a bill — the bank holds it as trustee until the purpose is fulfilled or fails (Barclays Bank v. Quistclose Investments, 1970 — the Quistclose trust); the same where the bank knows that the account is a trust account. On the bank's insolvency the beneficiary can trace the money; an ordinary depositor cannot."));
c.push(SEC("Lessor and lessee","Safe-deposit lockers hired for a rent; the bank is lessor, the customer lessee, with the qualifications in Amitabha Dasgupta."));
c.push(SEC("Mortgagee, pledgee, guarantee-holder","When the bank takes security for an advance it stands as mortgagee (Sec 58 Transfer of Property Act), pawnee (Sec 172 Contract Act) or the creditor under a guarantee (Sec 126) — Episode 04."));
c.push(SEC("Adviser, executor, attorney","Modern banks give investment advice (liability for negligent misstatement — Hedley Byrne v. Heller, 1964, and Woods v. Martins Bank, 1959), act as executors and trustees of wills, and hold powers of attorney."));
c.push(H3("The banker's duties"));
c.push(P("**(1) To honour cheques (Sec 31 NI Act):** the drawee of a cheque having sufficient funds of the drawer in his hands properly applicable to the payment of the cheque must pay it when duly required, and in default compensate the drawer for any loss or damage caused; conditions — sufficient funds, funds properly applicable (not earmarked, not attached, not subject to lien or set-off on another account), the cheque properly presented (at the branch, during hours, within three months of its date, not mutilated, in proper form), and no legal bar (garnishee order, death, insolvency, countermand, a court order). Wrongful dishonour of a trader's cheque entitles him to substantial damages without proof of special loss (Rolin v. Steward, 1854; Davidson v. Barclays Bank, 1940 — 'the smaller the amount, the greater the damage'); a non-trader must ordinarily prove actual loss, but Indian courts have awarded damages for injury to credit (Punjab National Bank v. Rajinder Kumar? — see the Consumer Protection Act cases on wrongful dishonour: Bank of Baroda v. Shri Sunderlal? ; the National Consumer Commission treats wrongful dishonour as deficiency in service). **(2) Secrecy:** the bank must not disclose the state of the customer's account or his affairs, a duty implied in the contract (**Tournier v. National Provincial and Union Bank of England, 1924** — the bank manager told the customer's employer that he was betting; the duty is qualified by four exceptions: disclosure under compulsion of law, duty to the public, the bank's own interest, and the customer's express or implied consent); in India the duty is statutory for public sector banks — Sec 13 of the Banking Companies (Acquisition and Transfer of Undertakings) Act 1970 obliges the bank 'to observe, except as otherwise required by law, the practices and usages customary among bankers' and not to divulge information relating to the affairs of constituents; the Banker's Books Evidence Act 1891 (Sec 4-6 — certified copies are evidence and the bank need not produce the books); statutory exceptions include the Income-tax Act (Sec 131, 133), the Prevention of Money-laundering Act 2002 (reporting), the Reserve Bank's credit-information rules (Credit Information Companies (Regulation) Act 2005 — the CIBIL system), the Companies Act, court orders, and the customer's consent for references; the Right to Privacy after K.S. Puttaswamy (2017) reinforces the duty, and the RBI's 2021 Digital Payments Security guidelines and the Digital Personal Data Protection Act 2023 add data-protection obligations. **(3) Care and skill:** in collecting, paying, advising and safe custody — the standard of a reasonably prudent banker (Sec 151 Contract Act; Sec 131 NI Act 'without negligence'). **(4) To render accounts:** pass book, statements; the duty to correct errors. **(5) To act on the mandate:** the bank may pay only as the customer directs; payment on a forged signature is no payment (Canara Bank v. Canara Sales Corporation). **(6) Notice before closing an account:** reasonable notice (Prosperity Ltd v. Lloyds Bank, 1923)."));
c.push(H3("The banker's rights and the customer's duties"));
c.push(P("Rights: **general lien** on securities deposited (Sec 171 Contract Act — Episode 04); **set-off** and combining accounts (Episode 03); **charging interest and commission** — interest at contractual or the bank's notified rates, compound interest with rests by custom (the RBI's interest-rate directives govern; Central Bank of India v. Ravindra, 2001 — capitalisation of interest at periodical rests is permissible if agreed or by usage, but interest on interest cannot be awarded by a court under Sec 34 CPC on the capitalised amount beyond the contract); **incidental charges** and the right to close an account on notice; **appropriation** where the customer does not (Episode 03); the right to demand repayment of an overdraft at any time in the absence of an agreement for a fixed period. The customer's duties: to draw cheques carefully so as not to facilitate fraud (London Joint Stock Bank v. Macmillan and Arthur, 1918 — a cheque drawn for two pounds with spaces that allowed alteration to one hundred and twenty; the customer bore the loss), to inform the bank promptly of known forgeries (Greenwood v. Martins Bank, 1933 — a husband who knew his wife was forging his cheques and said nothing was estopped), to present cheques within a reasonable time, and to pay interest and charges. Indian law on the customer's duty is narrower: **Canara Bank v. Canara Sales Corporation (1987)** held that a customer has no general duty to examine his statements and detect forgeries, and negligence of the customer is no defence to the bank unless it amounts to an estoppel in the Greenwood sense; **Bihta Co-operative Development and Cane Marketing Union v. Bank of Bihar (1967)** — the bank cannot debit the customer's account with a cheque bearing a forged endorsement unless protected by Sec 85. The RBI's Banking Codes and Standards Board (Code of Bank's Commitment to Customers), the Banking Ombudsman Scheme (now the Reserve Bank Integrated Ombudsman Scheme 2021) and the Consumer Protection Act 2019 give the customer remedies for deficiency in service."));
c.push(CASE("Foley v. Hill (1848) 2 HLC 28","Money deposited with a banker becomes the banker's own; the relationship is that of debtor and creditor, not trustee or agent; the banker must only repay an equivalent sum on demand."));
c.push(CASE("Joachimson v. Swiss Bank Corporation [1921] 3 KB 110","The terms of the banker-customer contract: repayment at the branch on written demand during banking hours; the customer must draw cheques with care; the bank must give reasonable notice before closing; demand is a condition precedent."));
c.push(CASE("Tournier v. National Provincial and Union Bank of England [1924] 1 KB 461","The banker's duty of secrecy is an implied term with four exceptions: compulsion of law, duty to the public, the bank's own interest, and the customer's consent."));
c.push(CASE("Shanti Prasad Jain v. Director of Enforcement AIR 1962 SC 1764","A deposit with a bank is a loan; the banker is a debtor and the customer a creditor; the relationship is not fiduciary."));
c.push(CASE("Canara Bank v. Canara Sales Corporation (1987) 2 SCC 666","A bank may debit a customer's account only on his mandate; payment on forged cheques is no payment; the customer's failure to check statements is not negligence that shifts the loss unless there is estoppel by knowledge and silence."));
c.push(DIG("The Foley principle now governs digital deposits too: a UPI or wallet balance held with a bank is a debt, but a prepaid-instrument balance held by a non-bank is escrowed under the Payment and Settlement Systems Act rules so that it is not the issuer's own money. The customer relationship begins with video KYC under the RBI's 2020 Master Direction, and the 'account' includes an e-wallet linked to a bank. The duty of secrecy meets the account-aggregator framework (RBI 2016, live since 2021): the customer's consent given through an aggregator app is the Tournier fourth exception in electronic form, and the Digital Personal Data Protection Act 2023 treats account data as personal data requiring lawful purpose. Wrongful dishonour of an electronic mandate (NACH/e-mandate) is treated like dishonour of a cheque by the Ombudsman."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Banking' under Sec 5(b), 'Customer', 'Banker's duty of secrecy', 'Foley v Hill', 'Wrongful dishonour of a cheque'. Part B: 'Discuss the general and special relationship between banker and customer' — debtor-creditor (Foley, Joachimson, Shanti Prasad Jain) with its features, then agent, bailee, trustee, lessor with one case each, then duties (Sec 31, secrecy with Tournier exceptions, care) and rights; or 'Explain the banker's duty of secrecy and its exceptions'. Part C: a bank tells a customer's employer about his overdraft — breach (Tournier) unless an exception applies; a bank dishonours a cheque although another account of the customer has funds — no duty to combine accounts unless agreed, but dishonour may be wrongful if the funds were in the same account; a bank pays a cheque on which the drawer's signature is forged — bank bears the loss (Canara Bank)."));
c.push(LN());

// ---------- EP 02 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 02 — The Minor Who Signed: Opening Accounts and the Special Customers"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Opening a new account — introduction, KYC, specimen signature · Kinds of accounts — current, savings, fixed and recurring deposits, joint accounts and survivorship · Special customers — minors, partnerships, companies, trusts, married women, pardanashin and illiterate persons, Hindu undivided families, clubs and societies, executors, liquidators, agents, NRIs"));
c.push(STORY("Calcutta, 1895. Dharmodas Ghose, a minor, mortgaged his property to the moneylender Brahmo Dutt for twenty thousand rupees, of which he received eight thousand. Brahmo Dutt's agent, Kedar Nath, knew from the boy's mother that he was under age. When Dharmodas sued through his mother to have the mortgage declared void, the moneylender's heirs argued that the boy had lied about his age and must at least return the money. In 1903 the Privy Council, in Mohori Bibee v. Dharmodas Ghose, laid down the rule that a minor's agreement is void ab initio — not merely voidable — because under Section 11 of the Contract Act a minor is not competent to contract at all; and since the lender knew the truth, there was no fraud on which restitution could be founded. That case is the reason every bank account opening form asks for the date of birth, and the reason Section 26 of the Negotiable Instruments Act says that a minor may draw, endorse, deliver and negotiate a promissory note, bill or cheque so as to bind all parties except himself. A bank may safely open a savings account for a minor, because a deposit received from a minor is money the bank owes and can repay; it may not lend to him, take a guarantee from him, or accept him as a partner, because any promise he makes is void. From the minor the law of accounts spreads to every other customer who is not a simple adult individual — the firm, the company, the trust, the married woman, the woman behind the veil, the man who cannot read, the joint family, the club, the executor — each with a rule about who may open, who may operate, and who may close."));
c.push(H3("Opening an account"));
c.push(P("The bank's precautions on opening an account are the foundation of the Sec 131 protection for collecting cheques 'without negligence': **introduction** by an existing customer or a respectable person, once the universal practice (the failure to obtain a proper introduction was negligence — Ladbroke v. Todd; Indian Overseas Bank v. Industrial Chain Concern, 1990), has been replaced by **Know Your Customer** norms under the RBI's Master Direction on KYC (2016, as amended) issued under the Prevention of Money-laundering Act 2002 and Rules 2005: officially valid documents (Aadhaar, passport, voter card, driving licence, NREGA card, NPR letter), Customer Due Diligence including video-based identification, the Central KYC Registry, periodic updating, and enhanced due diligence for politically exposed persons and high-risk accounts; **specimen signature** cards, or now signature images and biometric authentication; the **account-opening form** and the agreed terms (the RBI's fair-practice and Most Important Terms and Conditions requirements); the **nomination** facility under Sec 45ZA-45ZF of the Banking Regulation Act and the Banking Companies (Nomination) Rules 1985 — the nominee receives the deposit on the depositor's death as trustee for the legal heirs (Ram Chander Talwar v. Devender Kumar Talwar, 2010 — a nominee under Sec 45ZA is a receiver, not the owner, and the heirs may claim from him); and the mandatory **PAN or Form 60** for accounts and deposits above the prescribed limits. Under the Sec 131 case law the bank must make reasonable inquiries into the prospective customer's identity, occupation and the source of the first cheque, and a bank that opens an account without inquiry loses the protection when it collects a cheque to which the customer had no title."));
c.push(H3("Kinds of accounts"));
c.push(SEC("Current account","For businesses; no limit on transactions; no interest (or nominal); overdraft facility; cheque book; minimum balance; the account of the classic banker-customer cases."));
c.push(SEC("Savings account","For individuals and non-trading bodies; interest (deregulated by the RBI in 2011; each bank fixes it); restrictions on the number of withdrawals; basic savings bank deposit accounts (BSBDA — the 'no-frills' account under the financial-inclusion drive and the Pradhan Mantri Jan Dhan Yojana, 2014) with no minimum balance."));
c.push(SEC("Fixed (term) deposit","A sum for a fixed period at a fixed rate, evidenced by a receipt (the FDR — not a negotiable instrument, not transferable by endorsement); premature withdrawal at a penalty; loan against the deposit; the bank's lien and set-off on it (Syndicate Bank v. Vijay Kumar, 1992); interest is taxable and TDS applies; the deposit is repayable at maturity, and limitation runs from the demand after maturity."));
c.push(SEC("Recurring deposit","Monthly instalments for a fixed period; the sum with interest paid at maturity."));
c.push(SEC("Deposit account generally","Deposits are 'from the public' and insured by the Deposit Insurance and Credit Guarantee Corporation (DICGC Act 1961) up to five lakh rupees per depositor per bank (raised from one lakh in 2020 after the PMC Bank failure); interest on deposits is governed by the RBI's Interest Rate on Deposits Directions 2016; unclaimed deposits for ten years are transferred to the RBI's Depositor Education and Awareness Fund (Sec 26A BR Act, 2014; the UDGAM portal for tracing them)."));
c.push(SEC("Joint account","Opened by two or more persons with a mandate: 'either or survivor', 'former or survivor', 'jointly', or 'anyone or survivor'; the bank acts on the mandate; on the death of one holder the survivor may operate under an 'either or survivor' mandate, but the beneficial ownership of the balance depends on the intention of the parties, not on the mandate — the survivor may hold as trustee for the deceased's estate where the deceased alone contributed (Sec 45ZA nomination and the RBI's 2005 circular on settlement of claims); a joint account cannot be attached for the separate debt of one holder (Hirschorn v. Evans, 1938); a cheque drawn on a joint account must be signed as the mandate requires; the death or insolvency of one holder revokes an 'jointly' mandate; the RBI's 2005 guidelines permit 'either or survivor' term deposits to be paid to the survivor on maturity without the legal heirs' consent."));
c.push(H3("Special customers"));
c.push(SEC("Minor","Sec 3 of the Majority Act 1875 — eighteen years, or twenty-one where a guardian is appointed by a court; Sec 11 Contract Act; Mohori Bibee — agreements void; Sec 26 NI Act — a minor may draw, endorse, deliver and negotiate an instrument binding all parties except himself; Sec 68 Contract Act — necessaries supplied to a minor are recoverable from his property. Banking practice under the RBI's 2014 circular: a minor of any age may have a savings account operated by the natural guardian (father, then mother under the Hindu Minority and Guardianship Act 1956, Sec 6 — Githa Hariharan v. RBI, 1999, held that the mother is also a natural guardian and the bank cannot insist on the father); a minor above ten may open and operate a savings account independently subject to limits; no overdraft; on attaining majority the account is confirmed. A loan to a minor is irrecoverable; a guarantee by a minor is void; a minor as a partner (Sec 30 Partnership Act — admitted to the benefits only) cannot bind the firm to the bank."));
c.push(SEC("Partnership firm","Sec 4 of the Indian Partnership Act 1932; the account is opened in the firm's name on the application of all partners with the partnership deed and the registration certificate (registration is not compulsory but an unregistered firm cannot sue — Sec 69); the mandate specifies who may operate; **Sec 19(2)(a)-(h)** — a partner's implied authority does not extend to submitting a dispute to arbitration, opening a bank account on behalf of the firm in his own name, compromising a claim, withdrawing a suit, admitting liability, acquiring or transferring immovable property, or entering into partnership on behalf of the firm — so a partner cannot open the firm's account in his own name and the bank must not credit cheques payable to the firm into a partner's personal account (Sec 131 negligence — that is a classic examination point); a partner may draw cheques on the firm's account and may borrow for the firm's business in the ordinary course (Sec 19(1)); a cheque payable to the firm endorsed by a partner into his own account puts the bank on inquiry; on the death, insolvency or retirement of a partner the bank must stop the account to fix liability (Clayton's rule applies — Episode 03) and open a fresh one; the retiring partner remains liable for past debts unless a public notice is given (Sec 32, 45); a partner's authority may be restricted by agreement, but the restriction does not bind a bank without notice (Sec 20)."));
c.push(SEC("Company","A company is a separate legal person (Salomon v. Salomon, 1897); the bank requires the certificate of incorporation, the memorandum and articles, a certified copy of the **board resolution** authorising the opening and naming the operating officers, the list of directors, and the certificate of commencement (for public companies before 2015; now Sec 10A of the Companies Act 2013 declaration); the **ultra vires** doctrine (Ashbury Railway Carriage v. Riche, 1875) means a borrowing beyond the objects is void and the bank cannot recover, though it may trace the money — so the bank checks the borrowing power in the memorandum and, under Sec 180(1)(c) of the Companies Act 2013, the special resolution required for borrowing beyond the paid-up capital and free reserves; the **rule in Royal British Bank v. Turquand (1856)** — the doctrine of indoor management — protects the bank that deals with the company on the faith of its public documents against irregularities in the internal procedure (a resolution not actually passed), unless the bank had notice or the transaction was suspicious (Sec 176 Companies Act 2013 for defects in the appointment of directors); charges on the company's assets must be registered with the Registrar within thirty days under Sec 77 (formerly Sec 125 of the 1956 Act), failing which the charge is void against the liquidator and creditors, though the debt survives; a cheque payable to a company must not be credited to a director's personal account (negligence under Sec 131 — Nirmala? — Indian Overseas Bank v. Industrial Chain Concern); on winding up the account must be operated only by the liquidator."));
c.push(SEC("Trust","The Indian Trusts Act 1882 (private trusts) and the Charitable and Religious Trusts Act 1920, the Public Trusts Acts of the States, and the Wakf Act 1995; the bank obtains the trust deed, the resolution of the trustees, and the mandate; all trustees must act jointly unless the deed provides otherwise (Sec 48 Trusts Act — trustees cannot delegate, Sec 47); a trustee cannot use trust money for his own purposes, and a bank that with knowledge allows trust funds to be applied to the trustee's personal debt is liable for breach of trust (Sec 63; the bank's 'constructive notice' where the account is designated as a trust account); the bank has no set-off between a trust account and the trustee's personal account; on the death of a trustee the surviving trustees continue (Sec 76)."));
c.push(SEC("Married women","The Married Women's Property Act 1874 (Sec 4 — a married woman's wages and earnings and property acquired by her own skill are her separate property; Sec 7 — she may sue and be sued; Sec 8 — her husband is not liable for her ante-nuptial debts); a married woman has full contractual capacity, may open and operate an account and borrow; a loan to a married woman is recoverable only from her separate property (stridhan under Hindu law — Sec 14 of the Hindu Succession Act 1956 makes a Hindu woman the absolute owner of her property), not from her husband unless he is a party or she is his agent for necessaries; the bank should therefore lend to a married woman on the security of her own property and inquire into its ownership; the old practice of treating a married woman as a 'special' customer is now largely a matter of the husband's non-liability."));
c.push(SEC("Pardanashin and illiterate persons","A pardanashin woman is one who observes seclusion by custom; a contract with her is presumed to be induced by undue influence and the bank must prove that the transaction was explained to her and that she understood it (Sec 16 Contract Act; Kali Bakhsh v. Ram Gopal Singh, 1913; Tara Kumari v. Chandra Mauleshwar Prasad Singh, 1931); the bank should obtain the thumb impression attested by a person who explains the document, and an independent witness; an **illiterate person** is a competent customer, but documents must be read over and explained, the thumb impression attested by a witness known to the bank, a photograph kept, and no cheque book issued (withdrawal forms with the thumb impression in the presence of a bank official); the RBI's guidelines on accounts of visually impaired and illiterate customers (2008) require banks to offer all facilities, including ATM cards with assistance, and forbid discrimination."));
c.push(SEC("Hindu undivided family","The karta (manager) has implied authority to borrow for family necessity or benefit and to bind the family property (Hunooman Persaud Panday v. Mussumat Babooee Munraj Koonweree, 1856 — the lender must inquire into the necessity); the bank takes the account in the karta's name with a declaration by all adult coparceners of the constitution of the family and their concurrence; a coparcener's share may be liable; after the 2005 amendment daughters are coparceners and their signatures are obtained."));
c.push(SEC("Clubs, societies and associations","Unincorporated bodies have no legal personality; the account is opened in the name of the club on the resolution of the managing committee naming the office-bearers who may operate; the bank should not lend without the personal guarantee of the members because the club cannot be sued; registered societies under the Societies Registration Act 1860 sue and are sued through their office-bearers (Sec 6); co-operative societies are bodies corporate."));
c.push(SEC("Executors and administrators, liquidators, agents","The executor's authority flows from the will and the probate, the administrator's from the letters of administration (Sec 211 Indian Succession Act); the account is opened in the name of the estate; the executor may not mix estate money with his own or use it for his personal debt; a liquidator (Sec 290 Companies Act 2013; the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code) operates the company's account under the tribunal's supervision; an agent operates under a power of attorney which the bank examines for the extent of authority (a power to operate does not include a power to borrow or to overdraw unless expressly given), and the agency terminates on the principal's death or insanity (Sec 201 Contract Act) — the bank stops the account on notice of death."));
c.push(SEC("Non-resident Indians and foreign nationals","Accounts under the Foreign Exchange Management Act 1999 and the FEMA (Deposit) Regulations 2016: Non-Resident (External) Rupee accounts (NRE — repatriable, funded from abroad), Non-Resident Ordinary accounts (NRO — for income earned in India, limited repatriation), Foreign Currency Non-Resident (Bank) accounts (FCNR(B) — term deposits in foreign currency), and Resident Foreign Currency accounts on return; joint accounts with residents are permitted on a 'former or survivor' basis; the bank's authorised-dealer status under Sec 10 FEMA governs; Sec 6 FEMA capital-account transactions require RBI permission."));
c.push(CASE("Mohori Bibee v. Dharmodas Ghose (1903) 30 IA 114","A minor's agreement is void ab initio under Sec 11 of the Contract Act; no restitution where the lender knew of the minority; the basis of Sec 26 NI Act and of banks' refusal to lend to minors."));
c.push(CASE("Githa Hariharan v. Reserve Bank of India (1999) 2 SCC 228","The mother is a natural guardian of a minor along with the father; the RBI's form requiring only the father's signature to open an account for a minor was unconstitutional; 'after' in Sec 6 of the Hindu Minority and Guardianship Act means 'in the absence of'."));
c.push(CASE("Royal British Bank v. Turquand (1856) 6 E&B 327","A person dealing with a company in good faith is entitled to assume that its internal proceedings have been regularly conducted — the doctrine of indoor management on which banks rely when lending on board resolutions."));
c.push(CASE("Ram Chander Talwar v. Devender Kumar Talwar (2010) 10 SCC 671","A nominee under Sec 45ZA of the Banking Regulation Act receives the deposit on the depositor's death but does not become its owner; he holds it for the benefit of the legal heirs."));
c.push(CASE("Hirschorn v. Evans [1938] 2 KB 801","A joint account cannot be attached by a garnishee order for the separate debt of one of the account-holders."));
c.push(DIG("Account opening is now digital: video-KYC, Aadhaar e-KYC with OTP, DigiLocker documents, e-signature under the IT Act, and the Central KYC Registry mean that the 'introduction' has become an identity-verification record that a court will examine when the Sec 131 negligence question arises. Minors' accounts are opened online with the guardian's video consent; company accounts use MCA-verified board resolutions with digital signatures; and the RBI's 2023 direction on inoperative accounts requires banks to trace dormant customers electronically. Joint-account mandates and nominations are recorded in the core banking system and shown on the app, which is where the Talwar rule — the nominee is a trustee for the heirs — is now most often misunderstood."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Minor as a customer', 'Nomination', 'Joint account and survivorship', 'Partner's implied authority and bank accounts', 'Pardanashin woman', 'KYC'. Part B: 'Explain the precautions a banker should take in opening and operating accounts of special customers — minor, partnership, company, trust' — one paragraph each with the governing section (Sec 26 NI, Sec 19 Partnership, Turquand and Sec 77 Companies, Sec 47-48 Trusts) and the Sec 131 negligence link; or 'Describe the kinds of deposit accounts and the law relating to joint accounts'. Part C: a partner deposits a cheque payable to the firm into his personal account — bank negligent under Sec 131; a minor overdraws — irrecoverable; a nominee refuses to share the deposit with the heirs — Talwar; a company borrows beyond Sec 180 limits — voidable, bank should have checked the special resolution."));
c.push(LN());

// ---------- EP 03 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 03 — The Fixed Deposit and the Time-Barred Loan: Set-off, Appropriation and the Garnishee"));
c.push(H2("Covers: The pass book and statements · Overdraft · Bank drafts · Appropriation of payments and the rule in Clayton's case (Sec 59-61 Contract Act) · The right of set-off and combining accounts · Safe custody · Garnishee orders (Order XXI Rule 46, Sec 60 CPC)"));
c.push(STORY("Bihar, 1980s. Surendra Prasad Sinha stood guarantor for a loan taken by a friend from the Punjab National Bank. The friend defaulted, the bank did nothing for years, and the loan became time-barred. Meanwhile Sinha kept a fixed deposit of his own with the same bank. When the deposit matured the bank simply adjusted it against the guaranteed loan and paid him the balance. Sinha filed a criminal complaint against the bank's officers for misappropriation, and the High Court refused to quash it. The Supreme Court in 1992 quashed the prosecution and stated the law on set-off in a paragraph that every banking student memorises: the rule of limitation bars the remedy, not the right; a time-barred debt still exists; and a bank, being a creditor in possession of the debtor's money, may exercise its right of set-off — 'the right of a banker to combine accounts' — even against a time-barred debt, so long as the money is in its hands and the debtor is the same. The guarantor's liability being co-extensive with the principal debtor's, the bank could look to Sinha's deposit. The decision joins a chain that begins with a bank clerk's ledger in London in 1816: Clayton's case, which decides how payments into and out of a running account are appropriated, and which still determines who bears the loss when a partner dies, a guarantee is withdrawn, or a company is wound up. This episode is about the ledger: what the pass book proves, what an overdraft is, how a draft differs from a cheque, how payments are appropriated, when accounts may be combined, and what happens when a court's garnishee order reaches the account."));
c.push(H3("The pass book and statements"));
c.push(P("The pass book (now the statement of account, e-mail statement or app) is the bank's record of the account delivered to the customer. Its entries are **not conclusive**: the bank may correct an erroneous credit (though not, without notice, where the customer has altered his position in reliance — Holland v. Manchester and Liverpool District Banking Co., 1909; Skyring v. Greenwood, 1825 — an army agent who over-credited an officer for years could not recover after the officer had spent it; in India, Union Bank of India v. A.P. Bhonsle, 2004 Bombay? — banks may reverse wrong credits promptly) and the customer is not bound by a wrong debit merely because he did not object (the Canara Bank rule); under the **Bankers' Books Evidence Act 1891** a certified copy of an entry in a banker's book, including entries in electronic form (Sec 2(8), 2000 amendment), is prima facie evidence of the entry and of the matters recorded (Sec 4), and a bank need not produce the original books unless the court orders (Sec 5-6). A pass book entry may be an admission by the bank of the amount owed (Sec 18 Evidence Act) and may be an **acknowledgment** under Sec 18 of the Limitation Act if signed by the bank's officer. The customer's duty to examine the statement is not recognised in India as a duty whose breach shifts the loss of a forgery to him (Canara Bank v. Canara Sales Corporation), but prolonged silence with knowledge may found an estoppel."));
c.push(H3("Overdraft"));
c.push(P("An overdraft is a facility on a current account allowing the customer to draw beyond his credit balance up to an agreed limit; it is a loan repayable on demand (unless a period is agreed), attracts interest on the daily debit balance, and is usually secured; an unauthorised overdraft is a debt the bank may recover, and the bank's honouring of a cheque beyond the balance is a loan by implication, though the bank is not bound to honour it (Sec 31 — 'sufficient funds'); the RBI's guidelines on cash credit and overdraft (the Working Capital norms after the Tandon and Chore Committees, the loan system for delivery of bank credit, the 2018 guidelines on the drawing power) and the interest-rate framework (the marginal cost of funds based lending rate, then the external benchmark) apply; the limitation for a suit on an overdraft runs from the demand or, in a running account, from the last acknowledged balance (Art 1 — three years from the close of the year of the last item admitted, for a mutual, open and current account); interest is charged with periodic rests by usage (Central Bank of India v. Ravindra, 2001). Cash credit differs: a separate account with a limit against the security of stock or book debts, drawn as needed; a term loan is repayable by instalments; the three are the basic forms of bank advance."));
c.push(H3("Bank drafts and the appropriation of payments"));
c.push(P("A **demand draft** is an order to pay money drawn by one office of a bank on another office of the same bank (Sec 85A NI Act, 1930 — 'a draft drawn by one office of a bank upon another office of the same bank for a sum of money payable to order on demand'); it is a bill of exchange, not a cheque, though Sec 85A extends the paying banker's protection to drafts; it cannot be countermanded by the purchaser once delivered to the payee because the bank is both drawer and drawee and has received the consideration (the purchaser may request a stop before delivery, and the RBI's guidelines govern lost drafts and duplicates); it is payable on demand, may be crossed, and is a safer remittance than a cheque because the bank's own solvency stands behind it; a draft obtained by fraud on the bank may be stopped. **Appropriation of payments — Sec 59-61 of the Contract Act:** where a debtor owes several distinct debts to one creditor and makes a payment with an express or implied intimation that it is to be applied to a particular debt, it must be so applied (Sec 59); where the debtor does not intimate, the creditor may apply it at his discretion to any lawful debt actually due, even a time-barred one (Sec 60); where neither appropriates, the payment is applied in discharge of the debts in order of time, whether or not they are time-barred, and if of equal date, proportionately (Sec 61). **The rule in Clayton's case (Devaynes v. Noble, 1816):** in a running current account, in the absence of appropriation by either party, the first item on the debit side is discharged by the first item on the credit side — first in, first out. Consequences: on the death of a partner, the retirement of a guarantor, the notice of a second mortgage, or the customer's insolvency, the bank must **stop the account** (rule off the old account and open a new one); if it continues the old account, subsequent credits will be treated as paying off the old (secured or guaranteed) debt, and subsequent debits will create a new, unsecured debt (Deeley v. Lloyds Bank, 1912 — the bank that continued a single account after notice of a second mortgage lost the priority of its first mortgage). Indian courts apply Clayton's case as a rule of presumed intention, not an absolute rule (Bank of India v. Mehta Brothers, 1990? — the Supreme Court in Industrial Credit and Development Syndicate v. Smithaben H. Patel, 1999, held Sec 60-61 permit the creditor to appropriate first towards interest and then principal unless the contract provides otherwise; Gurpreet Singh v. Union of India, 2006 — the same principle applied to decrees; Meghraj v. Bayabai, 1969 — appropriation first to interest); a guarantor is discharged pro tanto by credits after the guarantee is withdrawn if the bank has not stopped the account."));
c.push(H3("Set-off and combining accounts"));
c.push(P("The **right of set-off** is the banker's right to adjust a debit balance in one account against a credit balance in another account of the same customer in the same right, so as to arrive at the net sum due — a species of the general right of a creditor in possession, recognised as an incident of the banker-customer relationship (Garnett v. McKewan, 1872 — the bank could dishonour a cheque drawn on one branch account because the customer's account at another branch was overdrawn; the customer must ask for the combined position; Halesowen Presswork and Assemblies v. National Westminster Bank, 1972 — the right exists unless excluded by agreement, and cannot be excluded on insolvency). Conditions: the accounts must be in the same name and in the same right (no set-off between a personal account and a trust or partnership account, nor between a joint account and an individual debt, nor between a customer's account and a debt due from him as guarantor unless the guarantee is called — but see Surendra Prasad Sinha); the debts must be mutual and ascertained; the debt must be due (a term loan not yet due cannot be set off against a deposit, unless the agreement so provides or the customer is insolvent); the bank should give notice, though notice is not required where the customer's cheque would otherwise be dishonoured or on his insolvency or death; the right may be varied by agreement (a letter of set-off or a lien letter is usually taken); a time-barred debt may be set off (Punjab National Bank v. Surendra Prasad Sinha, 1992). Statutory recognition: the Banking Regulation Act does not codify it, but Order VIII Rule 6 CPC allows legal set-off in a suit and equity permits set-off of connected claims; the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code Sec 36(4)? — mutual dealings and set-off under the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Board's regulations (Regulation 29) preserve the bank's set-off in liquidation. **Combining accounts** is the same right described from the other side: the bank may treat several accounts as one and refuse to honour a cheque on account A while account B is overdrawn beyond the combined balance — but it cannot combine accounts held in different rights, and an agreement to keep accounts separate (e.g. a loan account to be repaid by instalments) suspends the right until default. The customer has no corresponding right to insist on combination: he cannot draw on account A against a balance in a fixed deposit or a term account unless agreed."));
c.push(H3("Safe custody"));
c.push(P("A bank receiving valuables for safe custody is a **bailee** (Sec 148 Contract Act) bound to take reasonable care (Sec 151) and to return the goods to the bailor or according to his directions (Sec 160-161); it is liable for loss by its negligence or that of its servants, and for wrongful delivery to a person other than the bailor (conversion), even in good faith; it may charge a fee; it has no lien on the articles for the customer's general indebtedness (Sec 171 — general lien attaches only to securities deposited with the bank as a banker, not to articles left for safe keeping — Brandao v. Barnett, 1846; Sec 174 — the bailee's particular lien for charges applies); joint deposits are delivered on the joint mandate; on the death of the depositor the bank delivers to the legal representatives on production of probate or succession certificate, or under a nomination (Sec 45ZC-45ZE BR Act — nomination for articles in safe custody and for lockers); sealed packets are received without knowledge of the contents, which limits the standard of care to the packet. **Lockers** are hired under a lease with the bank as lessor (Amitabha Dasgupta, 2021, and the RBI's Safe Deposit Locker guidelines 2021 — a model agreement, the bank's liability for its own negligence up to one hundred times the annual rent, a register of access, and the bank's right to break open a locker for non-payment of rent after notice)."));
c.push(H3("Garnishee orders"));
c.push(P("A garnishee order is an order of a court, obtained by a judgment-creditor, attaching a debt due from a third person (the garnishee) to the judgment-debtor; a bank that owes its customer the credit balance is the garnishee. Under **Order XXI Rules 46 and 46A-46I CPC** the court first issues a **prohibitory order** (order nisi) restraining the bank from paying the debt and the customer from receiving it, and calling on the bank to show cause; then, if no cause is shown, an **order absolute** directing the bank to pay the amount into court. Rules: the order attaches only the balance actually standing to the customer's credit at the moment of service (Rogers v. Whiteley, 1892 — the whole balance is attached if the order does not specify a sum, so the bank must dishonour every subsequent cheque, even for small amounts; a later Indian practice, endorsed by the RBI, is that the bank may seek clarification and attach only the decretal amount if the order so limits); credits received after service are not attached (Heppenstall v. Jackson, 1939); cheques paid in for collection but not yet cleared are not attached; the order does not attach a fixed deposit not yet due unless the court so directs (the debt must be due or accruing due — a term deposit is 'accruing due' and attachable subject to maturity); a joint account cannot be attached for one holder's debt (Hirschorn v. Evans); a trust account cannot be attached for the trustee's debt; an account in a different right (partnership, HUF) is not the judgment-debtor's; property exempt under **Sec 60 CPC** — salary to the extent of the first one thousand rupees and two-thirds of the remainder, pensions, provident fund, life-insurance money — is not attachable even when it lies in the bank account? — the Supreme Court in Radhey Shyam Gupta v. Punjab National Bank (2009)? — the rule is that pension and provident fund money retain their exempt character in the bank account until the exemption is lost by mixing (Union of India v. Wing Commander R.R. Hingorani, 1987 — pension in the bank is not attachable; Radhey Shyam Gupta v. Punjab National Bank, 2009 — gratuity and retiral benefits credited to a bank account are exempt from attachment as they retain their character); the bank must obey the order and pay into court, and is discharged by the payment (Rule 46F); if the bank disputes liability the court tries the question (Rule 46C); the bank may set off its own dues before complying only if the set-off was exercised before service or the accounts were combined by agreement; the customer's insolvency after the order nisi but before the order absolute defeats the attachment in favour of the general body of creditors. The Income-tax Act (Sec 226(3)) and the GST law allow tax authorities to issue similar notices directly to banks, and the RBI's 2005? guidelines require banks to act on attachment orders of income-tax and court authorities and inform the customer."));
c.push(CASE("Devaynes v. Noble (Clayton's Case) (1816) 1 Mer 572","In a running account, absent appropriation, payments in are applied to the earliest debits — first in, first out; the reason banks rule off accounts on the death or retirement of a partner or guarantor."));
c.push(CASE("Garnett v. McKewan (1872) LR 8 Ex 10","A bank may combine a customer's accounts at different branches and dishonour a cheque on one where the other is overdrawn, without notice; the customer must inquire into the combined position."));
c.push(CASE("Punjab National Bank v. Surendra Prasad Sinha 1992 Supp (1) SCC 499","Limitation bars the remedy, not the right; a bank may set off a customer's deposit against a time-barred debt, including one guaranteed by him; the criminal complaint against the officers was quashed."));
c.push(CASE("Rogers v. Whiteley [1892] AC 118","A garnishee order attaches the whole credit balance at the time of service unless limited to a sum; the bank must refuse cheques thereafter."));
c.push(CASE("Radhey Shyam Gupta v. Punjab National Bank (2009) 1 SCC 376","Retiral benefits such as gratuity and provident fund deposited in a bank account retain their exempt character under Sec 60 CPC and cannot be attached or set off against the retired employee's guarantee liability."));
c.push(DIG("Garnishee and attachment orders now reach the bank as electronic notices under the income-tax and GST portals and, for court orders, through e-mail service under Order V Rule 9 with the bank's compliance filed online; the RBI's 2023 directions require freezing only the amount stated in the order. Set-off is exercised by automated 'sweep' and 'lien marking' in core banking systems — an FDR lien marked electronically has the same effect as the letter of set-off, and a court will ask whether the lien was marked before or after the garnishee order or the customer's death. Statements are electronic and the Bankers' Books Evidence Act's 2000 amendment makes a print-out with a Sec 2A certificate the 'certified copy'. Clayton's rule is applied by software when a guarantor's limit is withdrawn: the bank freezes the old account and opens a new one in the system on the same day."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Clayton's case', 'Right of set-off', 'Garnishee order', 'Bank draft vs cheque', 'Overdraft', 'Pass book entries as evidence'. Part B: 'Explain the banker's right of set-off and the conditions for its exercise, with reference to Surendra Prasad Sinha' — definition, Garnett v McKewan, the five conditions, the time-barred debt point, exclusions (different rights, trust, joint), the letter of set-off; or 'Discuss the rules of appropriation of payments under the Contract Act and the rule in Clayton's case and its application to banking'; or 'What is a garnishee order? Explain its effect on the banker'. Part C: a bank dishonours a cheque on the current account because the customer's loan instalment is overdue in another account — permissible only if the loan is due and the accounts are in the same right (set-off), otherwise wrongful dishonour; a garnishee order for ten thousand rupees is served on a bank holding fifty thousand — attach the decretal amount if the order limits it, else Rogers v Whiteley; a guarantor withdraws his guarantee and the bank continues the account — Clayton's rule reduces the guaranteed debt by later credits (Deeley)."));
c.push(LN());

// ---------- EP 04 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 04 — The Banker's Lien on the Fixed Deposit: Securities for Advances"));
c.push(H2("Covers: The banker's general lien (Sec 171 Contract Act) · Pledge (Sec 172-179) · Guarantee (Sec 126-147) · Documents of title to goods — bill of lading, dock warrant, warehouse-keeper's certificate, delivery order, railway receipt · Letters of credit and bank guarantees"));
c.push(STORY("Delhi, 1992. Vijay Kumar, a partner of Vijay Kumar and Co., had taken a loan from Syndicate Bank and the firm had also given fixed deposit receipts to the bank as security for a guarantee the bank had issued on its behalf. When the guarantee was invoked and the firm defaulted, the bank refused to release the fixed deposits and adjusted them. Vijay Kumar argued that the bank had no lien on a fixed deposit — that a fixed deposit was not a 'security' deposited with the bank but the bank's own debt to him, and Section 171 could not apply. The Supreme Court disagreed and explained the banker's general lien as it stands in Indian law: Section 171 gives a banker, in the absence of a contract to the contrary, a general lien on all securities deposited with him as a banker, for the general balance of the account; a fixed deposit receipt handed over to the bank as security is such a security; the lien extends to the money represented by it; and the lien is more than a right to retain — because the bank is a creditor in possession of the customer's own money, the lien combined with the right of set-off allows the bank to appropriate. The bank kept the deposits. This episode is about the securities on which banks lend: the lien that arises without any agreement, the pledge of goods and documents that the Contract Act regulates, the guarantee that makes a third person answerable, the documents of title that let goods in transit be pledged by handing over a piece of paper, and the two instruments — the letter of credit and the bank guarantee — by which banks lend their own credit to trade."));
c.push(H3("The banker's general lien — Sec 171 Contract Act"));
c.push(P("**Sec 171:** bankers, factors, wharfingers, attorneys of a High Court and policy-brokers may, in the absence of a contract to the contrary, retain as a security for a general balance of account any goods bailed to them; no other persons have a right to retain goods bailed to them as security for such balance unless there is an express contract to that effect. A **general lien** secures the general balance due, unlike the **particular lien** of Sec 170 (a bailee's right to retain goods on which he has spent labour, only for the charge for that work). The banker's lien is an **implied pledge** — 'the banker's lien is more than a mere right to retain; it is a lien coupled with a power of sale after reasonable notice' (Brandao v. Barnett, 1846, per Lord Campbell; adopted in India: Syndicate Bank v. Vijay Kumar, 1992; State Bank of India v. Javed Akhtar Hussain, 1993? — the Supreme Court in Vijay Kumar held that the bank may sell or appropriate). Conditions: the securities must have been deposited **with the bank as a banker** and **in the ordinary course of banking business** — not for safe custody, not for a specific purpose inconsistent with the lien (Brandao — exchequer bills left in a box for safe custody; a cheque handed over for collection with an instruction to credit a particular account; documents deposited to secure a specific loan which has been repaid, on which the lien may revive for the general balance — Re London and Globe Finance Corporation, 1902; a title deed deposited for a specific loan without agreement for a general lien); the lien is displaced by a contract to the contrary, express or implied from the circumstances; it does not extend to securities of a third party lodged for a specific purpose, to trust property known to be such, to money held in a different right, or to safe-custody articles; it covers negotiable instruments, share certificates, fixed deposit receipts, insurance policies, title deeds and goods received as a banker. Effect: the bank may retain, and, being a pledgee, sell after notice (Sec 176) or, in the case of money securities, appropriate; the lien is a security interest for the purposes of the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code and the SARFAESI Act (the Supreme Court's 2007 decision in Punjab National Bank v. Dr. Rajesh? — no; the leading modern case is **Syndicate Bank v. Vijay Kumar** and the RBI's guidelines that a bank must give notice before exercising a lien on a deposit, and cannot mark a lien on a deposit against a third party's dues without a letter). A **negative lien** is an undertaking by the borrower not to create a charge on his assets without the bank's consent; it creates no security interest but a personal obligation (and after 2016 is registrable with the Central Registry — CERSAI). The RBI requires banks to obtain a lien letter or a letter of set-off for deposits securing advances, and forbids exercising a lien on a deposit standing in a different name."));
c.push(H3("Pledge — Sec 172-179"));
c.push(P("**Sec 172:** the bailment of goods as security for payment of a debt or performance of a promise is a pledge; the bailor is the pawnor, the bailee the pawnee. Essentials: delivery of possession (actual or constructive — delivery of the key of the godown, or of the documents of title, or an attornment by a warehouseman holding the goods — Morvi Mercantile Bank v. Union of India, 1965; a pledge of goods 'in the pledgor's own custody' under a hypothecation is not a pledge), as security, of movable property (goods, documents of title, shares by transfer of the certificate with a blank transfer). **Sec 173:** the pawnee may retain the goods for the debt, interest and necessary expenses; **Sec 174:** not for any other debt unless there is a contract, but a subsequent advance is presumed to be secured; **Sec 175:** extraordinary expenses; **Sec 176 — the pawnee's remedies on default:** he may sue the pawnor on the debt and retain the goods as collateral, or sell after giving the pawnor reasonable **notice** of the sale — the notice is mandatory (Prabhat Kumar Mishra? — the Supreme Court in Lallan Prasad v. Rahmat Ali, 1967: the pawnee's right of sale is conditional on notice, and a pawnee who sells without notice or is unable to return the goods cannot sue for the debt without accounting for the goods; Hulas Kunwar v. Allahabad Bank, 1958 Calcutta; Central Bank of India v. Siriguppa Sugars, 2007 — the pawnee's rights prevail over the dues of unsecured creditors and over the sugarcane growers' statutory claims; Bank of Bihar v. State of Bihar, 1971 — the pawnee's rights are not defeated by the State's seizure of the pledged goods; the State must pay the bank); the surplus on sale goes to the pawnor and the deficit is recoverable; **Sec 177:** the pawnor may redeem at any time before the actual sale, even after the due date, on paying the debt and the expenses of the default; **Sec 178:** pledge by a mercantile agent in possession of goods or documents of title with the owner's consent, acting in the ordinary course, is valid if the pawnee acts in good faith without notice of the want of authority; **Sec 178A:** pledge by a person in possession under a voidable contract, before the contract is rescinded, is valid to a pawnee in good faith; **Sec 179:** pledge by a person with a limited interest is valid to the extent of that interest. The pawnee is not the owner — 'special property' passes to him, the general property remains with the pawnor (The Odessa, 1916; Lallan Prasad). **Hypothecation** — a charge on movables without possession, recognised by usage and now by Sec 2(n) of the SARFAESI Act 2002 — is the commoner form of security for stock and book debts; it is not a pledge, and the bank must take possession (under the deed or under Sec 13(4) SARFAESI) before selling; a hypothecation by a company is registrable under Sec 77 of the Companies Act."));
c.push(H3("Guarantee — Sec 126-147"));
c.push(P("**Sec 126:** a contract of guarantee is a contract to perform the promise or discharge the liability of a third person in case of his default; the surety, the principal debtor and the creditor; it may be oral or written. Essentials: three parties and two contracts (the principal contract and the guarantee), consideration — anything done or any promise made for the benefit of the principal debtor is sufficient consideration for the surety (Sec 127), including past advances if the guarantee is for a running account; no misrepresentation or concealment by the creditor (Sec 142-143 — a guarantee obtained by misrepresentation or by keeping silence as to a material circumstance is invalid). **Sec 128 — the surety's liability is co-extensive with that of the principal debtor** unless otherwise provided: the creditor may sue the surety without first suing the principal debtor or exhausting his remedies against him or against the security (Bank of Bihar v. Damodar Prasad, 1969 — a decree against the surety cannot be conditioned on first exhausting remedies against the principal; State Bank of India v. Indexport Registered, 1992 — a decree against the principal debtor, the mortgaged property and the guarantor may be executed against the guarantor first; Maharashtra State Electricity Board v. Official Liquidator, 1982 — the surety's liability continues though the principal debtor's is discharged by winding up; Industrial Investment Bank of India v. Biswanath Jhunjhunwala, 2009 — the creditor may proceed against the guarantor without exhausting remedies against the principal, subject to the terms). **Sec 129 — continuing guarantee** extends to a series of transactions (a guarantee for a cash-credit account); **Sec 130** it may be revoked as to future transactions by notice; **Sec 131** it is revoked by the surety's death as to future transactions; the liability for past transactions remains. **Discharge of the surety:** by any variance in the terms of the contract between the principal debtor and the creditor without the surety's consent (Sec 133 — even a variance that benefits the surety, though banks now take consent to variations in the guarantee itself); by the release or discharge of the principal debtor (Sec 134); by a composition with, or promise to give time to, the principal debtor without the surety's assent (Sec 135) — but not by mere forbearance to sue (Sec 137) nor by an agreement with a third person to give time (Sec 136); by the creditor's act or omission impairing the surety's eventual remedy — losing or releasing the security (Sec 139; State Bank of Saurashtra v. Chitranjan Rangnath Raja, 1980 — the bank's negligence in losing the pledged goods discharged the surety to the extent of their value; State of M.P. v. Kaluram, 1967); by the release of a co-surety only as to that co-surety (Sec 138). **The surety's rights:** on payment he is subrogated to the creditor's rights (Sec 140), entitled to the benefit of every security the creditor holds at the time of the guarantee whether or not he knew of it (Sec 141 — and if the creditor loses or parts with the security the surety is discharged to that extent), entitled to contribution from co-sureties (Sec 146-147), and entitled to indemnity from the principal debtor (Sec 145, an implied promise). Banking practice: guarantees are taken in the bank's form with clauses waiving Sec 133-139 (upheld — the sections apply 'in the absence of a contract to the contrary' — Sita Ram Gupta v. Punjab National Bank, 2008 — a clause that the guarantee is irrevocable is binding); guarantees by directors of a borrowing company are personal guarantees enforceable under the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code (Lalit Kumar Jain v. Union of India, 2021 — the guarantor's liability is not discharged by the approval of a resolution plan for the principal debtor); the limitation for a suit against a guarantor runs from the demand or the default as the guarantee provides (Art 55 or 113)."));
c.push(H3("Documents of title to goods"));
c.push(P("**Sec 2(4) of the Sale of Goods Act 1930:** 'document of title to goods' includes a bill of lading, dock warrant, warehouse-keeper's certificate, wharfinger's certificate, railway receipt, multimodal transport document, warrant or order for the delivery of goods, and any other document used in the ordinary course of business as proof of the possession or control of goods, or authorising or purporting to authorise, by endorsement or delivery, the possessor to transfer or receive the goods represented by it. Their importance to banks: transfer of the document by endorsement and delivery transfers the constructive possession of the goods, so the goods can be **pledged** while in transit or in a warehouse (Sec 178 Contract Act read with Sec 30 of the Sale of Goods Act); the bank advancing against a document of title is a pledgee of the goods (Morvi Mercantile Bank v. Union of India, 1965 — a railway receipt is a document of title, its endorsement and delivery to the bank as security is a pledge of the goods, and the bank as pledgee could sue the railway for the loss of the goods for their full value); the documents are not negotiable instruments — a transferee gets no better title than the transferor, except in the cases protected by Sec 178 and by the Bills of Lading Act 1856 for the bill of lading (a bill of lading is 'quasi-negotiable': its endorsement passes the property if so intended, Sec 1 of the Bills of Lading Act, but a thief cannot pass title). The individual documents: **bill of lading** — issued by the carrier by sea, a receipt for the goods, evidence of the contract of carriage, and the document of title; a 'clean' bill (no adverse remarks on the condition of the goods) is what a bank under a letter of credit demands; **dock warrant** — issued by a dock company certifying that goods are held and deliverable to the person named or his order; **warehouse-keeper's certificate** — the same for a warehouse, and under the Warehousing (Development and Regulation) Act 2007 a **negotiable warehouse receipt** issued by a registered warehouse is a fully negotiable document against which banks lend to farmers and traders (the e-NWR system); **delivery order** — an order by the owner to the holder of goods to deliver them to a named person, which becomes a document of title on attornment; **railway receipt** — issued by the railway under Sec 65 of the Railways Act 1989, transferable by endorsement, a document of title (Morvi Mercantile); **multimodal transport document** under the Multimodal Transportation of Goods Act 1993; and the **airway bill**, which is not a document of title (it is a receipt and contract, not transferable). Banks take these documents under **trust receipts** when releasing them to the borrower to take delivery and sell, the borrower holding the goods and proceeds in trust for the bank."));
c.push(H3("Letters of credit and bank guarantees"));
c.push(P("A **letter of credit** is an undertaking by a bank (the issuing bank), at the request of its customer (the applicant, usually the buyer), to pay the beneficiary (the seller) a sum of money against presentation of specified documents within a specified time — the commercial letter of credit is the principal mechanism of documentary credit in international trade and is governed by the International Chamber of Commerce's **Uniform Customs and Practice for Documentary Credits (UCP 600, 2007)** by incorporation, and in India by the Contract Act and the RBI's foreign-exchange regulations; kinds — revocable and irrevocable (UCP 600 makes all credits irrevocable), confirmed (a second bank adds its own undertaking) and unconfirmed, sight and usance (deferred payment or acceptance), transferable, back-to-back, revolving, red-clause (advance to the seller), standby (a guarantee in the form of a credit), and the inland letter of credit. Principles: the **autonomy of the credit** — the bank's obligation is independent of the underlying sale contract and of disputes about the goods (UCP Art 4-5: banks deal with documents, not goods), so the bank must pay against complying documents and cannot refuse because the goods are defective; **strict compliance** — the documents must conform to the terms of the credit (Equitable Trust Co. of New York v. Dawson Partners, 1927 — 'there is no room for documents which are almost the same'); and the **fraud exception** — the only ground on which a court will restrain payment is established fraud of the beneficiary of which the bank has notice (Sztejn v. J. Henry Schroder Banking Corporation, 1941; United City Merchants v. Royal Bank of Canada, 1983; in India, **U.P. Co-operative Federation v. Singh Consultants and Engineers (1988)** — the Supreme Court refused an injunction against encashment of an irrevocable letter of credit/bank guarantee except in a case of egregious fraud; Federal Bank v. V.M. Jog Engineering, 2001 — the issuing bank must honour a credit against conforming documents and the applicant's disputes are irrelevant; Tarapore and Co. v. V/O Tractoroexport, 1970). A **bank guarantee** is a contract of guarantee (Sec 126) in which the bank as surety undertakes to pay the beneficiary on the principal's default — in the common **unconditional or on-demand** form the bank undertakes to pay on the beneficiary's first demand without proof of default, and the law treats it like a letter of credit: the bank must pay on demand in terms of the guarantee, the court will not grant an injunction against invocation except for fraud of an egregious nature that vitiates the whole transaction or special equities in the form of irretrievable injustice (U.P. Co-operative Federation; Svenska Handelsbanken v. Indian Charge Chrome, 1994; Hindustan Steelworks Construction v. Tarapore and Co., 1996 — a bank guarantee is an independent contract and disputes under the underlying contract are no ground for injunction; Ansal Engineering Projects v. Tehri Hydro Development Corporation, 1996; Dwarikesh Sugar Industries v. Prem Heavy Engineering Works, 1997 — the court should not grant injunctions lightly; Himadri Chemicals Industries v. Coal Tar Refining Co., 2007 — a summary of the principles; Standard Chartered Bank v. Heavy Engineering Corporation, 2019 — the bank must honour an unconditional guarantee on invocation in terms, and a court examining only the invocation cannot go into the underlying dispute); kinds — performance guarantees, financial guarantees, bid bonds, advance-payment guarantees, deferred-payment guarantees; the guarantee must be invoked within its validity period, and Sec 28 of the Contract Act (as amended in 1997 and 2013, Exception 3) permits a bank guarantee to provide for the extinction of the bank's liability if no claim is made within a period of not less than one year after the guarantee period; the RBI's guidelines on guarantees and co-acceptances (the Master Circular) regulate the issue of guarantees, the margin, and the prohibition on guarantees for capital-market and inter-company deposits."));
c.push(CASE("Syndicate Bank v. Vijay Kumar (1992) 2 SCC 330","The banker's general lien under Sec 171 extends to fixed deposit receipts deposited as security; the lien is an implied pledge and the bank may appropriate the deposit against the customer's dues; the lien is not defeated by the deposit being the bank's own debt."));
c.push(CASE("Lallan Prasad v. Rahmat Ali AIR 1967 SC 1322","A pawnee who has sold the goods without notice, or cannot return them, cannot recover the debt without accounting for the goods; the pawnee's special property in the goods and the pawnor's right to redeem."));
c.push(CASE("Morvi Mercantile Bank Ltd. v. Union of India AIR 1965 SC 1954","A railway receipt is a document of title; its endorsement and delivery to a bank as security for an advance is a pledge of the goods; the bank as pledgee may sue the carrier for the full value of the goods lost."));
c.push(CASE("Bank of Bihar v. Damodar Prasad AIR 1969 SC 297","The surety's liability is co-extensive and immediate; the creditor need not first proceed against the principal debtor; a decree cannot direct the creditor to exhaust remedies against the principal before executing against the surety."));
c.push(CASE("U.P. Co-operative Federation Ltd. v. Singh Consultants and Engineers (P) Ltd. (1988) 1 SCC 174 and Hindustan Steelworks Construction Ltd. v. Tarapore and Co. (1996) 5 SCC 34","An unconditional bank guarantee or letter of credit must be honoured on demand irrespective of disputes under the underlying contract; a court will restrain encashment only for egregious fraud or irretrievable injustice."));
c.push(DIG("Security interests are now registered electronically: CERSAI (the Central Registry under Sec 20 SARFAESI) records mortgages, hypothecations and, since 2016, all security interests including pledges of receivables, and a search of CERSAI is the modern due diligence; the RBI's 2023 direction requires release of movable and immovable property documents within thirty days of repayment. Pledges of dematerialised shares are marked electronically with the depository (NSDL/CDSL pledge-repledge system under the SEBI 2020 framework). Warehouse receipts are electronic (e-NWR) under the Warehousing Development and Regulatory Authority. Letters of credit are issued and documents presented through SWIFT and the eUCP supplement to UCP 600 (2019) governs electronic presentation; bank guarantees are issued on the e-BG platform with the National e-Governance Services Limited's digital stamping (2022), which removes the forged-guarantee problem that Sec 131-type fraud cases used to produce."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Banker's lien', 'Pledge', 'Hypothecation', 'Continuing guarantee', 'Co-extensive liability', 'Documents of title to goods', 'Letter of credit', 'Bank guarantee', 'Trust receipt'. Part B: 'Explain the banker's general lien under Sec 171 with the conditions and exceptions, referring to Brandao v Barnett and Syndicate Bank v Vijay Kumar'; or 'Discuss the rights of a pawnee and the surety's rights and discharges under the Contract Act as they affect bank advances'; or 'What is a letter of credit? Explain the principles of autonomy and strict compliance and the fraud exception with Indian cases'. Part C: a bank sells pledged goods without notice — Lallan Prasad, the bank must account; a creditor gives the principal debtor six months' extra time without the surety's consent — surety discharged (Sec 135) unless the guarantee waives it; a buyer seeks an injunction against the bank paying under a letter of credit because the goods are substandard — refused (Singh Consultants); a bank lends against a bill of lading that was stolen — the bank gets no title (the bill is only quasi-negotiable)."));
c.push(LN());

// ---------- EP 05 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 05 — Paper That Passes Like Money: Kundan Lal Rallaram and the Negotiable Instrument"));
c.push(H2("Covers: The Negotiable Instruments Act 1881 · Definition and essential features of negotiable instruments (Sec 13) · Negotiability versus assignability · Promissory notes (Sec 4), bills of exchange (Sec 5), cheques (Sec 6) · Analogous instruments — bank drafts, traveller's cheques, dividend warrants, hundis · The presumptions (Sec 118-119) · Holder and holder in due course (Sec 8-9) and the privileges (Sec 36, 42, 43, 46, 53, 58-59, 120-122)"));
c.push(STORY("Delhi, after Partition. Kundan Lal Rallaram, a cloth merchant, sued the Custodian of Evacuee Property on a promissory note for some fourteen thousand rupees executed by a Muslim merchant who had left for Pakistan — the debt, he said, was the price of cloth he had supplied, and the promissory note was the merchant's promise to pay. The Custodian, who now held the evacuee's property, argued that no consideration had passed: the cloth had never been supplied. Kundan Lal answered that under Section 118 of the Negotiable Instruments Act the court must presume that every negotiable instrument was made for consideration, and it was for the Custodian to prove otherwise. The Supreme Court in 1961 explained how the presumption works: it shifts the burden of proof to the defendant, but the defendant may discharge it by evidence, direct or circumstantial — and, unusually, by the plaintiff's own failure to produce evidence that must have been in his possession: Kundan Lal had kept account books that would have shown the supply of cloth and did not produce them, and from that omission the court could infer that the books would not have supported him. The presumption was rebutted, and the suit failed. The case is the door into the Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881 — the law that lets a piece of paper pass from hand to hand carrying with it a promise to pay, free of the defences that would have defeated an ordinary assignment, and that supplies the presumptions of consideration, date, acceptance, endorsement and holding in due course on which the whole system of credit runs."));
c.push(H3("The Act and the concept of negotiability"));
c.push(P("The Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881 (Act XXVI of 1881) codified the English law merchant on bills, notes and cheques for India; it has been amended by the Banking Laws (Amendment) Act 1988 (Sec 138-142 dishonour of cheques, in force 1 April 1989), the Negotiable Instruments (Amendment and Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 2002 (electronic cheques, truncated cheques, Sec 143-147 summary trial and compounding), the 2015 amendment (Sec 142(2) jurisdiction), and the 2018 amendment (Sec 143A interim compensation, Sec 148 deposit in appeal). **Sec 13(1):** a 'negotiable instrument' means a promissory note, bill of exchange or cheque payable either to order or to bearer — the definition is not exhaustive, and by mercantile usage other instruments (hundis, government promissory notes, bearer debentures, share warrants, dividend warrants, railway receipts by custom in some markets, treasury bills) are negotiable (Sec 1 saves local usage relating to hundis). The **essential features**: (1) transferability by delivery (bearer) or by endorsement and delivery (order) — Sec 46-47; (2) the transferee who takes in good faith and for value (a holder in due course) gets a title free from the defects of title of prior parties — Sec 58 — the property of negotiability; (3) the holder may sue in his own name (Sec 8); (4) presumptions of consideration and regularity (Sec 118); (5) certainty of the sum and of the parties, unconditional promise or order, payable in money only. **Negotiability versus assignability:** an ordinary chose in action (a debt, a contract right) is transferred by assignment under Sec 130 of the Transfer of Property Act by a written instrument, notice to the debtor is needed to bind him (Sec 131), the assignee takes subject to all the equities and defences available against the assignor at the date of notice (Sec 132), and the assignee sues in his own name but must prove his title; a negotiable instrument is transferred by delivery or endorsement without a separate instrument, no notice is required, the holder in due course takes free of prior defects and defences (nemo dat quod non habet does not apply), consideration is presumed, and the holder sues in his own name on the instrument alone. 'Negotiation' (Sec 14 — transfer so as to constitute the transferee the holder) is thus a mode of transfer superior to assignment; a **'not negotiable' crossing** (Sec 130) removes the second feature while leaving transferability, and an instrument marked 'not transferable' or 'account payee' restricts it further."));
c.push(H3("The three instruments"));
c.push(SEC("Promissory note — Sec 4","An instrument in writing (not being a bank-note or a currency-note) containing an unconditional undertaking, signed by the maker, to pay a certain sum of money only to, or to the order of, a certain person, or to the bearer of the instrument. Essentials: in writing; an express promise, not a mere acknowledgment ('I owe you' is not a note; 'I promise to pay' is); unconditional (a promise to pay 'when able' or 'on the sale of the house' is not a note, but 'on demand' or at a fixed time or on the happening of an event certain to happen is); signed by the maker; certain parties (the maker and the payee must be certain — Sec 5? — the payee may be indicated by description); certain sum (with interest at a stated rate is certain; 'with such interest as may be due' is not); payable in money only; stamped under the Indian Stamp Act (Art 49 — an unstamped or insufficiently stamped note is inadmissible in evidence and cannot be validated by payment of the penalty, Sec 35 Stamp Act — the classic Part C trap); a note payable to bearer on demand cannot be made except by the RBI or the Central Government (Sec 31 RBI Act). Parties: maker, payee, and, after negotiation, endorsers and holders. Two persons may sign as joint makers (jointly and severally liable, Sec 43 Contract Act)."));
c.push(SEC("Bill of exchange — Sec 5","An instrument in writing containing an unconditional order, signed by the maker, directing a certain person to pay a certain sum of money only to, or to the order of, a certain person or to the bearer of the instrument. Essentials: an order, not a request; unconditional; three parties — the drawer (who gives the order), the drawee (to whom it is addressed and who becomes the acceptor on acceptance, Sec 7), and the payee; the drawee must be certain (Sec 5 Explanation? — a bill may be drawn on two drawees jointly, not in the alternative); the sum certain; payable on demand or after a fixed period (usance bill) — a bill payable 'after sight' matures three days after acceptance (days of grace, Sec 22 — abolished for cheques; the Act allows three days of grace for bills and notes not payable on demand); the acceptor's liability arises on acceptance (Sec 32); inland (Sec 11 — drawn and payable in India, or drawn in India on a person resident in India) and foreign bills (Sec 12 — drawn in sets, Sec 132-133); the bill may be drawn payable to the drawer's own order; presentment for acceptance (Sec 61) and for payment (Sec 64), noting and protest on dishonour (Sec 99-104 — protest is compulsory for foreign bills where the law of the place of drawing requires it); acceptance for honour (Sec 108-112) and payment for honour (Sec 113-114). The bill is the instrument of trade credit: a seller draws on the buyer, who accepts; the seller discounts the bill with a bank."));
c.push(SEC("Cheque — Sec 6","A bill of exchange drawn on a specified banker and not expressed to be payable otherwise than on demand, and (2002) includes the electronic image of a truncated cheque and a cheque in the electronic form. Explanation I: a 'cheque in the electronic form' is a cheque drawn in electronic form using any computer resource and signed in a secure system with a digital signature (with or without biometrics) and asymmetric crypto system (2015: or electronic signature); a 'truncated cheque' is a cheque truncated during the course of a clearing cycle, either by the clearing house or by the paying or collecting bank, immediately on generation of an electronic image for transmission, substituting the further physical movement of the cheque — the basis of the Cheque Truncation System (CTS) which has operated nationally since 2010-2014. A cheque differs from a bill: always drawn on a banker; always payable on demand; no acceptance (Sec 7 does not apply — a banker's 'marking' is a certification, not acceptance); no days of grace; may be crossed (Sec 123-131A); may be countermanded (Sec 31 read with Sec 90?); the drawer is discharged only if the holder's delay in presentment causes loss (Sec 84 — the drawer is discharged to the extent of the damage where the bank fails between the time the cheque should have been presented and the time it was); the banker is not liable to the holder (there is no privity — the holder's remedy is against the drawer and endorsers; the banker's duty under Sec 31 is to the drawer); statutory protection to the paying and collecting banker (Sec 85, 128, 131); the criminal remedy for dishonour (Sec 138). Post-dated cheques are cheques (Sec 6 read with Sec 138 — a post-dated cheque is a bill until its date, and the presumption of Sec 139 applies from the date; Anil Kumar Sawhney v. Gulshan Rai, 1993), stale cheques (older than three months from the date, by the RBI's 2011 direction reducing the period from six months) are not paid, ante-dated and mutilated cheques raise questions of alteration (Sec 87-89)."));
c.push(H3("Analogous instruments"));
c.push(P("**Bank draft** — Sec 85A: a draft drawn by one office of a bank on another office of the same bank is a bill of exchange; the paying banker is protected as for cheques; it cannot be stopped by the purchaser after delivery (Episode 03); a draft is 'payable to order' and may be crossed. **Traveller's cheque** — an instrument issued by a bank in fixed denominations, signed by the purchaser at issue and countersigned at encashment; not a cheque within Sec 6 (no drawee banker distinct from the drawer; payable on the purchaser's countersignature), but treated as a negotiable instrument by usage, and the issuing bank's undertaking is that of the maker of a promissory note; refund on loss is contractual. **Dividend warrant** — a company's order on its bank to pay a dividend to a shareholder; a cheque for the purposes of the paying banker's protection (Sec 85A? — the English Bills of Exchange Act Sec 95 treats it as a cheque; Indian practice does the same, and dividend warrants are crossed 'account payee'); after 2013 dividends are paid electronically or by 'dividend cheques'. **Interest warrant** — the same for interest on debentures or government securities. **Hundi** — the indigenous bill of exchange (darshani hundi — sight; muddati hundi — usance; shah-jog hundi — payable only to a respectable holder, a 'shah'; jokhmi hundi — conditional on the safe arrival of goods, and therefore not a negotiable instrument; nam-jog, dhani-jog, firman-jog) governed by local usage saved by Sec 1 and, where no usage is proved, by the Act. **Government promissory notes** and treasury bills are negotiable by the Public Debt Act 1944 / Government Securities Act 2006. **Bearer debentures** and **share warrants** are negotiable by usage. The **Reserve Bank's Payment Systems** — NEFT, RTGS, IMPS, UPI, NACH — are not negotiable instruments but electronic fund transfers governed by the Payment and Settlement Systems Act 2007 and the RBI's regulations; the Act's Sec 138 remedy does not apply to a failed e-transfer, only civil and consumer remedies do."));
c.push(H3("Presumptions — Sec 118-119"));
c.push(P("Until the contrary is proved, the following presumptions shall be made: (a) that every negotiable instrument was made or drawn for consideration, and that every such instrument when it has been accepted, endorsed, negotiated or transferred was accepted, endorsed, negotiated or transferred for consideration; (b) that every instrument bearing a date was made or drawn on such date; (c) that every accepted bill was accepted within a reasonable time after its date and before its maturity; (d) that every transfer was made before maturity; (e) that the endorsements appearing on it were made in the order in which they appear; (f) that a lost instrument was duly stamped; and (g) that the holder of a negotiable instrument is a holder in due course — provided that where the instrument was obtained from its lawful owner or custodian by means of an offence or fraud, or for an unlawful consideration, the burden of proving that the holder is a holder in due course lies on him. **Sec 119:** in a suit on an instrument which has been dishonoured, the court shall, on proof of the protest, presume the fact of dishonour. The presumptions are rebuttable presumptions of law ('shall presume', Sec 4 Evidence Act); they shift the burden of proof to the defendant, who may rebut by direct evidence, by circumstantial evidence, or by the plaintiff's own evidence and omissions (Kundan Lal Rallaram v. Custodian, Evacuee Property, 1961; Bharat Barrel and Drum Manufacturing Co. v. Amin Chand Payrelal, 1999 — once execution is admitted the presumption of consideration arises and the defendant must prove the absence of consideration on a preponderance of probabilities, though he may rely on the plaintiff's evidence; Rangappa v. Sri Mohan, 2010, on the parallel presumption under Sec 139 for cheques — the presumption includes the existence of a legally enforceable debt, and the accused may rebut it on a preponderance of probabilities; Basalingappa v. Mudibasappa, 2019; Bir Singh v. Mukesh Kumar, 2019 — a signed blank cheque filled in later attracts the presumption)."));
c.push(H3("Holder and holder in due course"));
c.push(P("**Sec 8 — holder:** any person entitled in his own name to the possession of the instrument and to receive or recover the amount due thereon from the parties thereto; a person who has lost the instrument or from whom it was stolen is still the holder as against the thief? — the Explanation? — the holder must have possession de jure (a thief or finder is not a holder; an agent in possession for his principal is not); the holder may sue in his own name, may negotiate, may give a valid discharge (Sec 82), may obtain a duplicate of a lost bill (Sec 45A). **Sec 9 — holder in due course:** any person who for **consideration** became the possessor of a promissory note, bill or cheque if payable to bearer, or the payee or endorsee thereof if payable to order, **before the amount mentioned in it became payable**, and **without having sufficient cause to believe that any defect existed in the title** of the person from whom he derived his title. Conditions: (1) for value — consideration actually given (a donee is not); (2) before maturity — a person who takes an overdue instrument takes it subject to defects (Sec 59); (3) in good faith and with due care — the Indian test 'without sufficient cause to believe' is stricter than the English 'in good faith without notice' (Sec 29 Bills of Exchange Act): negligence, or circumstances that would put a reasonable person on inquiry, defeat the status (U. Ponnappa Moothan Sons v. Catholic Syrian Bank, 1991 — a bank that took a cheque for collection without care was not a holder in due course); (4) the instrument must be complete and regular on its face (Sec 20 inchoate instruments — a holder in due course of a blank instrument filled in beyond the authority can enforce it for the amount filled in, subject to the stamp); (5) the holder must have obtained the instrument by lawful means. **Privileges of a holder in due course:** Sec 20 — inchoate instrument enforceable; Sec 36 — every prior party liable to him until satisfaction; Sec 42 — a bill drawn on a fictitious drawee or payable to a fictitious payee is enforceable by a holder in due course against the acceptor; Sec 43 — no defence of absence or failure of consideration against him; Sec 46 — a conditional or special-purpose delivery cannot be set up against him; Sec 53 — a holder deriving title from a holder in due course has the rights of the holder in due course (the 'shelter' rule); **Sec 58 — an instrument obtained by an offence, fraud or for an unlawful consideration cannot be enforced by the possessor or an endorsee, except a holder in due course**; Sec 59 — a person taking after maturity or with notice of dishonour has only the rights of the transferor; Sec 118(g) — the presumption; Sec 120 — no maker of a note, drawer of a bill or cheque, or acceptor of a bill payable to order shall in a suit by a holder in due course be permitted to deny the validity of the instrument as originally made or drawn; Sec 121 — no maker or acceptor may deny the payee's capacity to endorse; Sec 122 — no endorser may deny the signature or capacity of any prior party. The concept is central: negotiability exists for the holder in due course; everyone else takes an assignable debt."));
c.push(CASE("Kundan Lal Rallaram v. Custodian, Evacuee Property AIR 1961 SC 1316","The presumption of consideration under Sec 118 shifts the burden to the defendant, but it may be rebutted by circumstantial evidence including the plaintiff's failure to produce his own account books; the presumption is one of law, not fact."));
c.push(CASE("Bharat Barrel and Drum Manufacturing Co. v. Amin Chand Payrelal (1999) 3 SCC 35","Once execution of a promissory note is admitted, the Sec 118 presumption of consideration arises; the defendant must prove absence of consideration on a preponderance of probabilities, and may do so by relying on the plaintiff's case."));
c.push(CASE("U. Ponnappa Moothan Sons v. Catholic Syrian Bank Ltd. (1991) 1 SCC 113","The Indian test for a holder in due course — 'without sufficient cause to believe' that a defect existed — is stricter than the English good-faith test; a bank that collects a cheque without due care is not a holder in due course."));
c.push(CASE("Anil Kumar Sawhney v. Gulshan Rai (1993) 4 SCC 424","A post-dated cheque is a bill of exchange until the date written on it and becomes a cheque on that date; Sec 138 applies on presentation after that date."));
c.push(CASE("Bir Singh v. Mukesh Kumar (2019) 4 SCC 197","A signed blank cheque voluntarily handed over towards a payment attracts the presumption under Sec 139 even if the details are filled in by another; the drawer must rebut the presumption."));
c.push(DIG("The Act now recognises electronic cheques and truncated cheques (Sec 6 Explanations), and the Cheque Truncation System with 'positive pay' (the RBI's 2021 mandate for cheques above fifty thousand rupees — the drawer pre-confirms the details electronically) is the modern answer to alteration and forgery. Electronic signatures under the IT Act (Sec 3A) can sign an electronic cheque. The presumptions of Sec 118 apply to electronic instruments once the electronic record is proved under Sec 63 BSA. Digital promissory notes are being tested on the RBI's regulatory sandbox and the Sec 31 RBI Act bar on bearer demand notes applies to them. UPI and IMPS transfers are not negotiable instruments — a failed transfer is a payment-system dispute under the PSS Act and the Ombudsman scheme, not a Sec 138 offence — a distinction that Part C problems now test."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Negotiable instrument', 'Negotiability vs assignability', 'Holder in due course', 'Promissory note', 'Post-dated cheque', 'Truncated cheque', 'Presumptions under Sec 118', 'Hundi'. Part B: 'Define a negotiable instrument and explain its essential characteristics; distinguish negotiability from assignability'; or 'Who is a holder in due course? What are his privileges?' — Sec 9 conditions with Ponnappa Moothan, then the privileges section by section; or 'Distinguish a promissory note, a bill of exchange and a cheque' — a table of ten points. Part C: an instrument reading 'I owe you Rs 5,000' — not a note; a cheque taken for a debt after the payee knew it was stolen — not a holder in due course, Sec 58; a defendant admits signing a note but says no money was received — the Sec 118 presumption applies and he must rebut (Bharat Barrel); a note payable to bearer on demand executed by a trader — void under Sec 31 RBI Act."));
c.push(LN());

// ---------- EP 06 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 06 — Not Negotiable: Great Western Railway and the Anatomy of a Cheque"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Kinds of cheques · Crossing — general, special, 'account payee', 'not negotiable' (Sec 123-131A) · Endorsement and its kinds (Sec 15-16, 50-56) · Holder and holder in due course of a cheque · Payment in due course (Sec 10) · Marking of cheques · Countermand, and when a banker must refuse payment"));
c.push(STORY("London, 1900. Huggins, a rate collector employed by a local council, received cheques from ratepayers payable to the council and crossed 'not negotiable'. He was not a customer of the London and County Bank, but for years its Ealing branch had cashed such cheques for him over the counter as a favour — he would hand in the cheque, the bank would pay him the cash and collect the cheque from the drawee. Huggins embezzled the money. The Great Western Railway Company, one of the ratepayers whose cheque he had cashed, sued the bank for conversion. The House of Lords held the bank liable: the bank had not 'received payment for a customer' — Huggins had no account and was not a customer, so the statutory protection for the collecting banker did not apply — and, the cheque being crossed 'not negotiable', the bank could get no better title than Huggins, who had none. The case teaches three things at once: what a crossing does, what 'not negotiable' means, and why a bank that handles a crossed cheque for a stranger does so at its peril. From it this episode dissects the cheque: the kinds of cheques, the crossings and their legal effect, the endorsements that move an order cheque from hand to hand, the meaning of payment in due course which is the paying banker's shield, the old practice of marking a cheque 'good for payment', and the circumstances in which the banker must refuse to pay at all."));
c.push(H3("Kinds of cheques"));
c.push(P("**Bearer** cheque — payable to bearer, or to a named person 'or bearer'; transferable by mere delivery; the bank pays whoever presents it (Sec 85(2) protects the banker who pays a bearer cheque in due course notwithstanding any endorsement); once a bearer, always a bearer (Sec 85(2) Explanation — an endorsement in full does not convert it). **Order** cheque — payable to a named person 'or order', or to a named person without the words 'or bearer' (Sec 13 Explanation (i) — an instrument payable to a specified person is payable to his order); transferable by endorsement and delivery; the paying banker must be satisfied of the regularity of the endorsement (Sec 85(1)). **Open** cheque — uncrossed, payable at the counter. **Crossed** cheque — payable only through a banker. **Post-dated** — bears a future date; payable only on or after that date; a bill of exchange until then (Anil Kumar Sawhney); the bank that pays before the date does so at its risk. **Ante-dated** — bears a past date; valid. **Stale** — presented after the validity period (three months from the date since 2012 by the RBI direction; the drawer may revalidate). **Mutilated** — torn or damaged; the bank returns it unless the drawer confirms. **Blank or inchoate** cheque — signed but the amount or payee left blank; the holder has authority to fill it (Sec 20). **Self** cheque — payable to 'self', drawn by the customer for cash. **Banker's cheque or pay order** — issued by a bank on itself for local payment; **Multi-city cheque** — payable at par at any branch under the RBI's CTS-2010 standards; **Electronic** and **truncated** cheques (Sec 6). **Cheque with a limiting endorsement** 'not exceeding Rs' — the bank pays up to the limit. **Gift cheques**."));
c.push(H3("Crossing — Sec 123-131A"));
c.push(P("A crossing is a direction to the paying banker to pay the cheque only through a banker. **Sec 123 — general crossing:** where a cheque bears across its face an addition of the words 'and company' or any abbreviation between two parallel transverse lines, or two parallel transverse lines simply, with or without the words 'not negotiable' — the cheque is crossed generally; **Sec 124 — special crossing:** where a cheque bears across its face an addition of the name of a banker, with or without 'not negotiable' — crossed specially to that banker; **Sec 125 — crossing after issue:** the holder may cross an uncrossed cheque generally or specially, may convert a general crossing to a special one, and may add 'not negotiable'; a banker to whom a cheque is crossed specially may cross it again to another banker as his agent for collection; **Sec 126 — payment of crossed cheque:** a generally crossed cheque shall not be paid otherwise than to a banker, and a specially crossed cheque otherwise than to the banker to whom it is crossed or his agent for collection; **Sec 127:** where a cheque is crossed specially to more than one banker (except an agent for collection) the banker on whom it is drawn shall refuse payment; **Sec 128 — payment in due course of a crossed cheque:** where the banker on whom a crossed cheque is drawn has paid it in due course, the banker paying and (if the cheque has come to the hands of the payee) the drawer shall respectively be entitled to the same rights and be placed in the same position as if the amount had been paid to and received by the true owner; **Sec 129 — payment out of due course:** a banker paying a crossed cheque otherwise than in accordance with Sec 126 is liable to the true owner for any loss he may sustain; **Sec 130 — 'not negotiable' crossing:** a person taking a cheque crossed generally or specially, bearing the words 'not negotiable', shall not have and shall not be capable of giving a better title to the cheque than that which the person from whom he took it had — the cheque remains transferable but loses negotiability; the transferee takes subject to defects of title (Great Western Railway v. London and County Bank; Wilson and Meeson v. Pickering, 1946 — a 'not negotiable' cheque given to a clerk to fill in for a specific purpose and misappropriated could not be enforced by the transferee); **Sec 131 — collecting banker's protection** (Episode 07); **Sec 131A:** the crossing provisions apply to drafts. **'Account payee' crossing** is not defined in the Act (except by the 2002 amendment's Explanation to Sec 131, which deems a banker collecting an account-payee cheque for someone other than the named payee to be negligent) — it is a direction to the collecting banker to credit only the payee's account, recognised by usage (National Bank v. Silke, 1891; Akrokerri Mines v. Economic Bank, 1904 — the crossing does not affect the paying banker, who cannot know into whose account the cheque is credited); the RBI's 1992 direction (after the securities scam) requires banks to credit account-payee cheques only to the payee's account and forbids collection for third parties (with a limited exception for co-operative credit societies up to fifty thousand rupees), and the CTS-2010 standard prints 'a/c payee' on cheque leaves; an account-payee cheque is not transferable in banking practice, though the Act does not make it so in law (a payee may not endorse it to another and expect a bank to collect it). Opening of crossing: only the drawer may cancel a crossing, by writing 'pay cash' and signing in full; a bank should refuse a cheque whose crossing appears cancelled otherwise. Effect of crossing on the drawer: a crossing is not a countermand and does not affect the drawer's liability. Crossing protects the true owner by making the cheque traceable through a bank account; a 'not negotiable' crossing protects him further by depriving the thief's transferee of a good title; an 'account payee' crossing protects him best by confining the credit to the payee."));
c.push(H3("Endorsement — Sec 15-16, 50-56"));
c.push(P("**Sec 15:** when the maker or holder of a negotiable instrument signs the same, otherwise than as such maker, for the purpose of negotiation, on the back or face thereof or on a slip of paper annexed thereto (an 'allonge'), or so signs for the same purpose a stamped paper intended to be completed as a negotiable instrument, he is said to endorse the same and is called the endorser. Essentials: on the instrument or an allonge; signed by the holder (or his agent — Sec 27-28); for the purpose of negotiation; delivery (Sec 46 — an endorsement without delivery is not complete). **Sec 16 — kinds:** endorsement **in blank** — the endorser signs his name only; the instrument becomes payable to bearer (Sec 54 — a blank endorsement makes the instrument payable to bearer even though originally payable to order); endorsement **in full** (special) — the endorser adds a direction to pay to a specified person or his order; **Sec 49:** the holder of an instrument endorsed in blank may convert it into an endorsement in full by writing above the signature a direction to pay to another person; **Sec 50:** the endorsement, followed by delivery, transfers to the endorsee the property in the instrument with the right of further negotiation, but the endorsement may restrict or exclude the right (**restrictive endorsement** — 'pay A only', 'pay A for my use', 'pay A or order for collection'); **Sec 52 — sans recourse endorsement:** the endorser may exclude his own liability by 'sans recourse' or 'without recourse to me', or make it conditional (**conditional endorsement** — 'pay A on his marriage'; the condition binds the endorser and the endorsee but not the paying banker, who may pay disregarding it — Sec 52? — the paying banker's position is protected by Sec 85); **facultative endorsement** — the endorser waives some right, e.g. 'notice of dishonour waived'; **Sec 56 — partial endorsement:** an endorsement purporting to transfer only a part of the amount is invalid for the purpose of negotiation (but where the instrument has been paid in part, a note of the part payment may be endorsed and the residue negotiated); **Sec 55:** conversion of an endorsement in blank into an endorsement in full; **Sec 51:** every sole maker, drawer, payee or endorsee, or all of several joint makers, may endorse and negotiate — but the maker or drawer cannot endorse unless he is the holder; **Sec 53:** a holder deriving title from a holder in due course; **Sec 40-41:** the endorser's liability is discharged if the holder destroys or impairs his remedy against a prior party; **Sec 35:** the endorser's liability — he is bound to compensate every subsequent holder if the instrument is dishonoured, provided he receives due notice of dishonour (Sec 93); **Sec 45A:** duplicates. Rules of practice for endorsement: the endorsement must be in the same name and spelling as the payee's (a misspelt payee endorses first in the misspelt form and then correctly); a married woman endorses in her own name; an agent endorses 'for and on behalf of' with his authority (Sec 27-28 — an agent signing without indicating that he signs as agent is personally liable); an executor endorses as such; a firm's endorsement is by a partner in the firm name (Sec 19 Partnership Act); a company's by its authorised officer with the seal or under the board's authority; a minor's endorsement binds all except himself (Sec 26). The order of endorsements is presumed (Sec 118(e)); a forged endorsement is a nullity and no title passes (Sec 58 read with the general law; the paying banker's protection under Sec 85 for order cheques is the statutory exception)."));
c.push(H3("Payment in due course — Sec 10"));
c.push(P("'Payment in due course' means payment **in accordance with the apparent tenor of the instrument**, **in good faith and without negligence**, to any person in possession thereof under circumstances which do not afford a reasonable ground for believing that he is not entitled to receive payment of the amount therein mentioned. Elements: (1) according to the apparent tenor — the amount, the date (not before a post-dated cheque's date), the payee (not a cancelled crossing), the currency; (2) in good faith and without negligence — the ordinary care of a prudent banker: examining the signature against the specimen, the date, the crossing, the endorsements' regularity, any material alteration, whether the amount in words and figures agree (Sec 18 — the words prevail), whether there is a countermand or a garnishee order or notice of death; (3) to a person in possession — the bearer, or the apparent endorsee; (4) under circumstances not affording reasonable ground for suspicion — payment of a large sum over the counter to a stranger, or of a cheque with a shaky signature, or one presented long after date, may put the bank on inquiry. A banker who pays in due course is discharged (Sec 82(c) — the maker, acceptor or endorser is discharged by payment in due course; Sec 85 — the paying banker's protection is expressed in terms of payment in due course; Sec 128 for crossed cheques). Payment out of due course — on a forged drawer's signature (never in due course, because the mandate is absent; Canara Bank), after countermand, after notice of death, on a materially altered cheque that is apparent, against a stopped or attached account, or to a person whose title is suspect — makes the bank liable to the drawer (wrongful debit) or the true owner (conversion)."));
c.push(H3("Marking of cheques and when the banker must refuse payment"));
c.push(P("**Marking** — the paying banker, at the request of the holder or the collecting banker, writes 'good for payment' on a cheque, indicating that funds are available and are earmarked; the Act does not provide for it (a cheque cannot be 'accepted'), but by usage a cheque marked at the request of the holder binds the banker to pay, and a cheque marked at the request of the drawer or another bank for clearing is a representation that may found an estoppel — the practice is obsolete in India since the RBI discouraged it (the demand draft and the banker's cheque replace it); its legal effect where it survives: the marking bank is estopped from dishonouring for want of funds, but the drawer remains liable and may countermand? — the RBI's view is that marking should be confined to cheques for clearing purposes. **When the banker must refuse payment:** on **countermand** (stop payment) by the drawer (Sec 31? — the customer's mandate is revoked; the order must be clear and reach the paying branch before payment; a countermand after payment is ineffective; a stop-payment cheque may still found a Sec 138 prosecution — Modi Cements); on notice of the **death** of the customer (the mandate ends; cheques presented after death are returned 'drawer deceased' even if dated before); on notice of the customer's **insanity**; on the customer's **insolvency** — the presentation of a petition (Sec 28 Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code moratorium; the earlier Presidency Towns and Provincial Insolvency Acts); on receipt of a **garnishee order** or an attachment order of a court or tax authority; on notice of the **winding up** of a company customer (the liquidator alone may operate); where the cheque is **post-dated** and presented before the date, or **stale**; where the signature **differs** from the specimen; where the cheque is **materially altered** without the drawer's authenticated confirmation (Sec 87); where the amount in **words and figures differ** (Sec 18 — the bank may pay the amount in words, but practice is to return); where the cheque is **mutilated**; where the crossing is **irregular** or a specially crossed cheque is presented through the wrong bank (Sec 126-127); where the account is **closed** or the customer's funds are **insufficient** (the cheque is returned 'insufficient funds' — the ground for Sec 138; NEPC Micon v. Magma Leasing, 1999 — 'account closed' is within Sec 138); where the customer has **assigned** the balance or given notice of a trust; where the cheque is drawn on **another branch** without a multi-city arrangement; where the drawer's **authority** as agent, partner, director or trustee is doubtful or has terminated; where the cheque is **irregularly endorsed** (Sec 85 — the banker must ensure the endorsement is regular, though he need not verify its genuineness); where the bank has received notice of a **loss** of the cheque or **fraud** (the bank may refuse pending inquiry); and where the cheque is presented on a **holiday** or outside hours. The bank may refuse but is not bound to refuse where the customer has drawn a cheque for a purpose the bank thinks unwise, or where a third party asserts a claim without a court order."));
c.push(CASE("Great Western Railway Co. v. London and County Banking Co. [1901] AC 414","A bank that cashes a crossed 'not negotiable' cheque for a person who is not a customer is not protected as a collecting banker and, taking under a 'not negotiable' crossing, gets no better title than the presenter; liable in conversion."));
c.push(CASE("Wilson and Meeson v. Pickering [1946] KB 422","A cheque crossed 'not negotiable' handed to a clerk for a limited purpose and misappropriated could not be enforced by the transferee against the drawer, because the 'not negotiable' crossing meant the transferee took subject to the clerk's defective title."));
c.push(CASE("National Bank v. Silke [1891] 1 QB 435","An 'account payee' crossing is a direction to the collecting banker and does not affect the negotiability of the cheque or the paying banker's position — the origin of the usage that Indian banks and the 2002 amendment to Sec 131 now enforce."));
c.push(CASE("Modi Cements Ltd. v. Kuchil Kumar Nandi (1998) 3 SCC 249","A cheque dishonoured on a 'stop payment' instruction attracts Sec 138 if issued for a legally enforceable debt; the drawer cannot escape by countermanding (overruling Electronics Trade and Technology Development Corporation v. Indian Technologists)."));
c.push(CASE("NEPC Micon Ltd. v. Magma Leasing Ltd. (1999) 4 SCC 253","'Account closed' is a dishonour within Sec 138; the words 'the amount of money standing to the credit of that account is insufficient' include a closed account."));
c.push(DIG("The CTS-2010 cheque carries the crossing and 'a/c payee' pre-printed, and the image-based clearing means the paying bank sees only an image: the RBI's positive-pay system requires the drawer to upload the cheque's details for high-value cheques, and the paying bank matches the image against them — 'apparent tenor' is now checked by software. Endorsements have nearly vanished from cheques because account-payee crossing is universal; the law of endorsement survives mainly for bills, drafts and warehouse receipts. Countermands are given through the app or net-banking and take effect instantly across branches; death notices are flagged in the core banking system; and dishonour memos are generated with the standard return reason codes, which is how the Sec 138 'insufficient funds' or 'account closed' reason is proved in court."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Crossing of cheques', 'Not negotiable crossing', 'Account payee crossing', 'Endorsement in blank and in full', 'Sans recourse endorsement', 'Partial endorsement', 'Payment in due course', 'Marking of cheques', 'Countermand'. Part B: 'What is crossing? Explain the kinds of crossing and their legal effect with reference to Sec 123-131' — general, special, not negotiable (Great Western Railway, Wilson v Pickering), account payee (Silke and the RBI direction), who may cross, Sec 126-129; or 'Define endorsement and explain its kinds with the rules of a valid endorsement'; or 'When is a banker justified in refusing payment of a customer's cheque?'. Part C: a specially crossed cheque is presented through a different bank — refuse (Sec 126); a cheque endorsed 'pay A for collection' is endorsed by A to B — restrictive endorsement, B gets no title; a customer stops a cheque after the payee has supplied goods — bank must refuse, drawer faces Sec 138 (Modi Cements); a thief transfers a 'not negotiable' cheque to an innocent purchaser for value — the purchaser gets no title (Sec 130)."));
c.push(LN());

module.exports=c;
